# Acorn deploy

Kevin tests Acorn on this Mac. He does not choose staging vs stable channels.

When he says **deploy**, **push update**, or **ship**:

1. Commit every non-secret change (never `.env`, updater private keys, or credentials).
2. Push the current branch.
3. Actually ship the Mac app people install — do not stop at opening a PR.

When writing `latest.json`, include the matching Windows NSIS installer:

```bash
node apps/desktop/src-tauri/scripts/write-acorn-latest-json.mjs \
  --version <version> --tag v<version> --repo kvnpyy/acorn-releases \
  --notes "Acorn <version>" \
  --windows-artifact path/to/Acorn_<version>_x64-setup.exe
```

The `.exe.sig` must sit next to the installer. Do not bump the feed version without that same-version Windows binary — a Mac-only newer `latest.json` makes Windows clients try to update and fail. Do not pass `--allow-missing-windows` unless he explicitly asks for a Mac-only feed.

Staging/stable in GitHub Actions is leftover Anarlog wiring. For Acorn, ship the production Mac build (the Action input is still `channel: stable` if that workflow is used). Do not explain those channel names unless he asks.

A PR is not a deploy. GitHub check failures are GitHub tests, not his local app.
